  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the crystal structure of a layered nickelate, highlighting how different atomic species and metal orbitals are arranged in the material. In the central panel, yellow, blue, and red spheres represent Nd, Ni, and O atoms, respectively. The structure appears as stacked Ni–O layers separated by Nd ions, emphasizing a quasi-two-dimensional network built from corner-sharing oxygen coordination around nickel.\n\nTwo dashed insets connect the lattice to schematic orbital shapes on selected Ni sites. One inset shows the \\(d_{x^2-y^2}\\) orbital, with lobes lying in the plane of the Ni–O network, while the other shows the \\(d_{z^2}\\) orbital, elongated along the vertical direction with a ring around the center. Together, these callouts indicate the two key Ni \\(e_g\\) orbitals and their different spatial orientations relative to the crystal axes.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the relationship between the layered atomic structure and the directional character of Ni electronic states. The main scientific point is that the geometry of the lattice distinguishes in-plane and out-of-plane orbital character, which is important for understanding the material’s bonding and electronic behavior.",
